<figure class="segment-paths">
  <div class="figure-label">One catch-up request, two storage planes</div>

  <div class="paths">
    <article class="path before">
      <header>
        <span class="tag">Before</span>
        <strong>Every history page comes from the Redis frame ZSET</strong>
      </header>

      <div class="flow" aria-label="Old Redis sorted set read path">
        <div class="node client">
          <span>Client</span>
          <b>GET from offset</b>
          <small>one logical snapshot</small>
        </div>
        <span class="connector" aria-hidden="true">↓</span>
        <div class="node chronicle">
          <span>Chronicle</span>
          <b>bounded page loop</b>
          <small>decode and write frames</small>
        </div>
        <span class="connector" aria-hidden="true">↓</span>
        <div class="node redis">
          <span>Redis primary</span>
          <div class="zset" aria-hidden="true">
            <i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i>
          </div>
          <b>metadata + frame ZSET</b>
          <small>serves the complete replay, page by page</small>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="work">
        <span>Append authority</span>
        <strong>Redis</strong>
        <span>Historical read bytes</span>
        <strong>Redis</strong>
      </div>

      <p>
        Bounded catch-up limits each page, but Redis still stores and returns
        every historical frame. More Chronicle replicas do not remove that
        replay work from the Redis primary.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="path after">
      <header>
        <span class="tag">After</span>
        <strong>Verified segments serve the sealed prefix</strong>
      </header>

      <div class="flow" aria-label="Immutable segment serving read path">
        <div class="node client">
          <span>Client</span>
          <b>same GET from offset</b>
          <small>the HTTP contract is unchanged</small>
        </div>
        <span class="connector" aria-hidden="true">↓</span>
        <div class="node chronicle">
          <span>Chronicle</span>
          <b>snapshot + manifest pin</b>
          <small>one incarnation and upper tail</small>
        </div>
        <span class="connector fork" aria-hidden="true">↙ &nbsp; ↘</span>
        <div class="split">
          <div class="node segments">
            <span>Immutable origin</span>
            <div class="blocks" aria-hidden="true">
              <i></i><i></i><i></i><i></i>
            </div>
            <b>sealed prefix</b>
            <small>authenticated 64 KiB blocks</small>
          </div>
          <div class="node redis">
            <span>Redis primary</span>
            <div class="tail" aria-hidden="true">
              <i></i><i></i><i></i>
            </div>
            <b>snapshot + hot tail</b>
            <small>still validates liveness</small>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="work">
        <span>Append authority</span>
        <strong>Redis</strong>
        <span>Sealed read bytes</span>
        <strong>segments</strong>
      </div>

      <p>
        Chronicle reads the immutable prefix in bounded authenticated ranges,
        then drains the Redis hot tail under the first snapshot. Missing or
        corrupt segment data falls back to that captured Redis snapshot.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <figcaption>
    <strong>The change moves sealed read work, not append truth.</strong>
    Redis still acknowledges every append, retains the complete frame ZSET,
    controls expiry and deletion, and supplies the bounded hot tail.
  </figcaption>
</figure>

